WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.359
Welcome to the first topic on the RCP 203 final study guide.

2
00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:08.760
It packs five exam items into one chapter.

3
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:19.530
We start with pneumonia: what it is, how it shows up on exam, and how the categories and the typical versus atypical patterns differ.

4
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:30.381
Then we read a chest X-ray step by step, and use those findings to recognize a pleural effusion and tell a transudate from an exudate.

5
00:00:30.381 --> 00:00:34.997
Next come the accessory muscles and what their use means.

6
00:00:34.997 --> 00:00:45.200
We finish with the weaning and extubation numbers, and the separate checks that decide whether the tube can actually come out.

7
00:00:46.600 --> 00:01:01.993
The RCP 103 deck defines pneumonia as an infectious inflammatory process that mainly affects the gas exchange area of the lungs, causing capillary fluid to pour into the alveoli.

8
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:06.317
Causes are bacteria, viruses, fungi or aspiration.

9
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:13.841
Hess calls it inflammation and consolidation of lung tissue, most often from infection.

10
00:01:13.841 --> 00:01:20.154
Vital signs rise: fever, heart rate, respiratory rate and blood pressure.

11
00:01:20.154 --> 00:01:31.050
The decks separate fever by cause, above one hundred one degrees Fahrenheit for bacterial and below one hundred one for viral.

12
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:39.871
Tachypnea comes from hypoxemia sensed by the peripheral chemoreceptors, and from poor lung compliance.

13
00:01:39.871 --> 00:01:51.200
The cough starts dry and hacking, then turns productive with yellow or green sputum, and it may be rust colored or show hemoptysis.

14
00:01:52.600 --> 00:02:05.495
Over consolidated lung, the exam shows increased vocal fremitus, a dull percussion note, bronchial breath sounds or crackles, and whispered pectoriloquy.

15
00:02:05.495 --> 00:02:11.226
In lobar pneumonia, chest expansion is reduced on the affected side.

16
00:02:11.226 --> 00:02:22.519
The chest X-ray shows increased density, air bronchograms, and possibly an abscess with an air fluid level, or an effusion or empyema.

17
00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:32.296
The blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis with hypoxemia early, and respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia when severe.

18
00:02:32.296 --> 00:02:45.022
The white count rises in bacterial pneumonia and falls in viral pneumonia, sputum goes for culture and sensitivity, and the PFT pattern is restrictive.

19
00:02:45.022 --> 00:02:49.152
Hess adds that pneumonia is a clinical diagnosis.

20
00:02:49.152 --> 00:02:57.327
An infiltrate on imaging is necessary, and a pathogen is isolated in only forty percent of cases.

21
00:02:57.327 --> 00:03:04.575
Bronchoalveolar lavage is indicated for non-resolving pneumonia and for bacterial VAP.

22
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.735
The decks sort pneumonia by where it was acquired.

23
00:03:10.735 --> 00:03:23.237
Community acquired pneumonia comes from normal social contact, and Hess names Streptococcus pneumoniae as the most common bacterium.

24
00:03:23.237 --> 00:03:33.750
Hospital acquired pneumonia appears forty eight hours or more after admission and was not present at admission.

25
00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:39.527
Its main organisms are Pseudomonas aeruginosa, MSSA and MRSA.

26
00:03:39.527 --> 00:03:45.020
Ventilator associated pneumonia develops after intubation.

27
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:59.416
The sources disagree on timing: RCP 103 says more than forty eight to seventy two hours, while the RCP 105 notes and RCP 203 both say forty eight hours.

28
00:03:59.416 --> 00:04:02.257
Use forty eight hours or more.

29
00:04:02.257 --> 00:04:17.790
Health care associated pneumonia meant hospitalization within ninety days, nursing home residence, or IV antibiotics, chemotherapy or wound care within thirty days.

30
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.525
Hess says that term was retired in twenty sixteen.

31
00:04:23.933 --> 00:04:26.389
Hess contrasts two patterns.

32
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:33.755
Typical pneumonia starts abruptly, with rigors, high fever and pleuritic chest pain.

33
00:04:33.755 --> 00:04:46.998
The sputum is rusty or purulent, the white count is usually elevated, and the chest X-ray shows lobar or segmental consolidation with air bronchograms.

34
00:04:46.998 --> 00:04:51.032
The main organism is Streptococcus pneumoniae.

35
00:04:51.032 --> 00:04:58.223
Atypical pneumonia is a flu-like illness: dry cough, moderate fever and no rigors.

36
00:04:58.223 --> 00:05:01.205
It is more common in young adults.

37
00:05:01.205 --> 00:05:10.150
The white count is usually normal, and the film shows diffuse interstitial or peribronchial opacities.

38
00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:17.868
Its organisms, such as Mycoplasma, Chlamydia and Legionella, are not seen on Gram stain.

39
00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:24.883
Mycoplasma is the most common atypical cause, and it produces walking pneumonia.

40
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.564
Pause and work this one out.

41
00:05:28.564 --> 00:05:33.577
A young adult has a dry cough, a moderate fever and no rigors.

42
00:05:33.577 --> 00:05:40.287
The white count is normal, and the chest film shows diffuse interstitial opacities.

43
00:05:40.287 --> 00:05:49.100
Which pattern is this, what is its most common cause, and what drug class does the deck tell you to think of?

44
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.000
[6-second practice pause]

45
00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.656
This is atypical pneumonia.

46
00:05:58.656 --> 00:06:11.835
The flu-like picture, the missing rigors, the normal white count and the diffuse interstitial film all point away from the abrupt lobar picture of typical pneumonia.

47
00:06:11.835 --> 00:06:18.144
The most common atypical cause is Mycoplasma, which produces walking pneumonia.

48
00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:25.492
Because these organisms are not seen on Gram stain, a negative stain does not rule them out.

49
00:06:25.492 --> 00:06:33.000
For treatment, the pharmacology deck gives a simple rule: atypical pneumonia, think macrolide.

50
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.172
RCP 203 gives a fixed order.

51
00:06:37.172 --> 00:06:44.399
First verify the patient identity, the orientation and the image quality.

52
00:06:44.399 --> 00:06:47.171
Identify the view, AP or PA.

53
00:06:47.171 --> 00:06:54.200
Check the hemidiaphragms and the costophrenic angles, sharp or blunted.

54
00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.972
Check the tracheal position.

55
00:06:56.972 --> 00:07:00.337
Estimate the cardiothoracic ratio.

56
00:07:00.337 --> 00:07:02.812
Note every tube and line.

57
00:07:02.812 --> 00:07:06.079
Then compare with previous films.

58
00:07:06.079 --> 00:07:16.672
For image quality, remember the word ripe: rotation, inspiration, position, meaning AP or PA, and exposure.

59
00:07:16.672 --> 00:07:19.246
Exposure is a common trap.

60
00:07:19.246 --> 00:07:26.275
An underexposed film is too white, and an overexposed film is too dark.

61
00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:32.106
Think of white as more stuff and black as more air.

62
00:07:32.106 --> 00:07:42.214
An opacity, meaning white, points to atelectasis, consolidation or pneumonia, pulmonary edema, or a pleural effusion.

63
00:07:42.214 --> 00:07:47.743
A lucency, meaning black, points to emphysema or a pneumothorax.

64
00:07:47.743 --> 00:07:53.014
A blunted costophrenic angle means fluid, a pleural effusion.

65
00:07:53.014 --> 00:07:56.210
The diaphragm tells you about volume.

66
00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:03.294
A flattened diaphragm means hyperinflation, as in COPD, emphysema or acute asthma.

67
00:08:03.294 --> 00:08:12.625
An elevated diaphragm means volume loss or diaphragm weakness, as in atelectasis or phrenic nerve paralysis.

68
00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:21.405
Cardiomegaly means the heart is wider than fifty percent of the chest on a PA film.

69
00:08:21.405 --> 00:08:28.333
Do not apply that rule to an AP film, because the AP view magnifies the heart.

70
00:08:28.333 --> 00:08:34.551
Kerley B lines mean interstitial pulmonary edema, as in heart failure.

71
00:08:34.551 --> 00:08:41.478
The endotracheal tube tip should sit four to six centimeters above the carina.

72
00:08:41.478 --> 00:08:46.630
A tube that is too deep usually enters the right mainstem.

73
00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.117
Hess adds three film tricks.

74
00:08:49.117 --> 00:08:54.002
A lateral decubitus film identifies free pleural fluid.

75
00:08:54.002 --> 00:08:58.088
An expiratory film shows a small pneumothorax.

76
00:08:58.088 --> 00:09:07.058
And an air bronchogram means the opacity is in the lung itself, not in the pleura or the mediastinum.

77
00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:12.763
A pleural effusion is fluid in the pleural space.

78
00:09:12.763 --> 00:09:24.775
The common causes are CHF, cancer and pneumonia, and it is a restrictive disorder because it limits lung expansion and lowers compliance.

79
00:09:24.775 --> 00:09:27.230
Effusions come in two kinds.

80
00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:31.614
A transudate comes from systemic pressure changes.

81
00:09:31.614 --> 00:09:39.242
The fluid is thin and watery, low in protein, with few cells, and clear or light straw.

82
00:09:39.242 --> 00:09:45.204
CHF is the most common cause, then cirrhosis and nephrotic syndrome.

83
00:09:45.204 --> 00:09:52.043
An exudate comes from pleural inflammation that raises capillary permeability.

84
00:09:52.043 --> 00:09:57.566
The fluid is high in protein with many cells, cloudy or opaque.

85
00:09:57.566 --> 00:10:04.668
Causes are infection such as pneumonia or TB, malignancy, and autoimmune disease.

86
00:10:04.668 --> 00:10:11.157
The deck says a fluid pH below seven point three zero suggests an exudate.

87
00:10:11.157 --> 00:10:22.292
Hess, using Light's criteria, gives a fluid to serum protein ratio of point five or more, or an LDH ratio of point six or more.

88
00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:28.414
A large effusion pushes the trachea away from the fluid.

89
00:10:28.414 --> 00:10:37.084
Tactile fremitus is decreased, the percussion note is dull, and breath sounds are diminished or absent.

90
00:10:37.084 --> 00:10:44.828
On the chest X-ray, blunting of the costophrenic angle is the earliest and most common sign.

91
00:10:44.828 --> 00:10:52.067
Larger effusions add a meniscus sign and shift the mediastinum to the unaffected side.

92
00:10:52.067 --> 00:10:58.801
Effusions under about three hundred milliliters may not show on a standard film.

93
00:10:58.801 --> 00:11:11.091
Thoracentesis can be diagnostic, to tell transudate from exudate, or therapeutic, when the effusion is large enough to cause respiratory distress.

94
00:11:11.091 --> 00:11:15.720
Up to about fifteen hundred milliliters may be removed.

95
00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:22.875
Complications are pneumothorax, bleeding, infection and re-expansion pulmonary edema.

96
00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:36.565
The accessory muscles of inspiration, per RCP 203, are the sternocleidomastoid, the scalenes, the upper trapezius and the pectoralis major.

97
00:11:36.565 --> 00:11:40.271
Hess also lists the external intercostals.

98
00:11:40.271 --> 00:11:43.007
Expiration is normally passive.

99
00:11:43.007 --> 00:12:02.154
The expiratory accessory muscles, the internal intercostals and the abdominals, join in when resistance to expiration is significant, as in severe obstructive disease, so abdominal contraction means active exhalation.

100
00:12:02.154 --> 00:12:10.361
The deck's main point is that accessory muscle use at rest means increased work of breathing.

101
00:12:10.361 --> 00:12:20.596
During a spontaneous breathing trial, it is a sign to consider stopping, and it is an early sign of weaning failure.

102
00:12:20.596 --> 00:12:36.303
The NBRC pearl: a rate of thirty eight, accessory muscle use, sweating, a falling tidal volume and a rising carbon dioxide mean impending ventilatory failure from muscle fatigue.

103
00:12:36.303 --> 00:12:40.450
One trap: paralysis masks accessory muscle use.

104
00:12:41.867 --> 00:12:49.249
The rapid shallow breathing index is the rate divided by the tidal volume in liters.

105
00:12:49.249 --> 00:12:52.764
Use less than one hundred, from RCP 105.

106
00:12:52.764 --> 00:13:01.201
Hess uses less than one hundred five, and the deck says the NBRC commonly uses one hundred five.

107
00:13:01.201 --> 00:13:15.525
Negative inspiratory force should be minus twenty to minus thirty centimeters of water in twenty seconds, and RCP 203 says minus twenty is the minimum to extubate.

108
00:13:15.525 --> 00:13:19.128
Normal is more negative than minus sixty.

109
00:13:19.128 --> 00:13:30.113
Vital capacity should be greater than ten milliliters per kilogram, with fifteen, about one liter, in some notes and in Hess.

110
00:13:30.113 --> 00:13:39.517
Spontaneous tidal volume should be at least five milliliters per kilogram as the general weaning predictor.

111
00:13:40.933 --> 00:13:45.984
Minute ventilation is normally six to ten liters per minute.

112
00:13:45.984 --> 00:13:52.129
Above ten is often linked to poor weaning outcomes, and Hess uses twelve.

113
00:13:52.129 --> 00:14:07.113
For oxygenation, RCP 203 wants a P over F ratio of one hundred fifty to two hundred or more, PEEP of five to eight or less, and inspired oxygen of forty to fifty percent or less.

114
00:14:07.113 --> 00:14:14.353
The pH should be at least seven point two five to screen, and compliance above thirty.

115
00:14:14.353 --> 00:14:49.708
After at least thirty minutes, RCP 203 calls the trial passed when saturation stays at least eighty five to ninety percent, the pH stays at least seven point three zero, carbon dioxide rises no more than ten, the rate stays at or below thirty to thirty five, heart rate below one hundred twenty to one hundred forty, systolic pressure between ninety and one hundred eighty to two hundred, and no vasopressors are needed.

116
00:14:51.133 --> 00:14:54.995
RCP 203 also teaches the NHLBI ARDS protocol.

117
00:14:54.995 --> 00:15:08.211
A patient is ready for a trial on inspired oxygen of forty percent or less with PEEP of eight or less, or fifty percent or less with PEEP of five or less.

118
00:15:08.211 --> 00:15:17.050
The trial lasts up to one hundred twenty minutes on a T-piece, a trach collar, or CPAP of five or less.

119
00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:37.903
It is tolerated when saturation is at least ninety percent, the tidal volume is at least four milliliters per kilogram of predicted body weight, the rate is thirty five or less, the pH at least seven point three zero, and there is no distress.

120
00:15:37.903 --> 00:15:42.193
Tolerated for thirty minutes, consider extubation.

121
00:15:42.193 --> 00:15:43.652
Here is the trap.

122
00:15:43.652 --> 00:15:54.808
Answer five milliliters per kilogram for a general weaning predictor, and use four only when the question names the ARDS protocol.

123
00:15:56.233 --> 00:15:57.828
Try the calculation.

124
00:15:57.828 --> 00:16:08.590
During a spontaneous breathing trial, your patient breathes at a rate of thirty eight with a tidal volume of three hundred milliliters.

125
00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:12.018
Work out the rapid shallow breathing index.

126
00:16:12.018 --> 00:16:16.083
Is this patient ready, and what should happen next?

127
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:23.000
[6-second practice pause]

128
00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:30.510
Convert the tidal volume to liters first, three hundred milliliters is point three.

129
00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:36.423
Thirty eight divided by point three is about one hundred twenty seven.

130
00:16:36.423 --> 00:16:41.068
That is above one hundred, so the patient is not ready.

131
00:16:41.068 --> 00:16:47.825
When a trial fails, find the cause and repeat the trial every twenty four hours.

132
00:16:47.825 --> 00:17:00.325
Notice too that a rate of thirty eight with a falling tidal volume is part of the NBRC picture of impending ventilatory failure from muscle fatigue.

133
00:17:01.733 --> 00:17:07.170
Passing the trial and pulling the tube are two separate decisions.

134
00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:16.148
The chapter thirteen deck says the extubation decision should be made separately from the weaning assessment.

135
00:17:16.148 --> 00:17:32.210
Before the trial comes a daily sedation interruption, the sedation vacation, called a spontaneous awakening trial in some hospitals, for every ventilated patient stable enough to be off sedation.

136
00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:37.317
It allows a more accurate readiness and neurologic assessment.

137
00:17:37.317 --> 00:17:42.506
If the trial is not tolerated, return to the previous settings.

138
00:17:42.506 --> 00:17:44.730
Then four things must hold.

139
00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:46.954
The upper airway is patent.

140
00:17:46.954 --> 00:17:54.532
The airway can be protected, with a gag reflex and the ability to lift the head off the bed.

141
00:17:54.532 --> 00:18:00.792
There is an adequate cuff leak, with no sign of significant airway swelling.

142
00:18:00.792 --> 00:18:03.263
And secretions can be cleared.

143
00:18:03.263 --> 00:18:07.958
The most serious extubation complication is laryngospasm.

144
00:18:09.367 --> 00:18:26.599
Clearing secretions means a deep cough when suctioned and, if the patient is alert, a maximum expiratory pressure above sixty centimeters of water, a peak cough flow above sixty liters per minute, or both.

145
00:18:26.599 --> 00:18:29.289
Peak cough flow has three bands.

146
00:18:29.289 --> 00:18:33.492
Above one hundred sixty is a normal, strong cough.

147
00:18:33.492 --> 00:18:41.561
Sixty to one hundred sixty is weak but possibly adequate, a borderline cough that may need help.

148
00:18:41.561 --> 00:18:50.051
Below sixty is inadequate, and the patient is not ready because secretions are likely to be retained.

149
00:18:50.051 --> 00:18:58.710
Normal expiratory strength is plus eighty to plus one hundred fifty, so sixty is a floor, not a target.

150
00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:08.376
One caution: the same slide says SIMV is no longer recommended for weaning, then adds that you may be tested on it.

151
00:19:08.376 --> 00:19:10.142
Know it for the exam.

152
00:19:11.567 --> 00:19:14.699
Here is what to carry into the exam.

153
00:19:14.699 --> 00:19:24.791
Pneumonia is a clinical diagnosis that needs an infiltrate, and atypical pneumonia means Mycoplasma and a macrolide.

154
00:19:24.791 --> 00:19:30.533
Read every film in the same order and check its quality with ripe.

155
00:19:30.533 --> 00:19:36.536
White is more stuff, black is more air, and a blunted angle is fluid.

156
00:19:36.536 --> 00:19:42.713
A transudate is a pressure problem, an exudate an inflammation problem.

157
00:19:42.713 --> 00:19:48.194
Accessory muscle use at rest means increased work of breathing.

158
00:19:48.194 --> 00:20:03.767
For weaning, the RSBI must be under one hundred, the general tidal volume at least five milliliters per kilogram, and passing the trial is not the same as being ready to extubate.
